{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-142964",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $Y$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{10}{3}x{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3}{y}^{4}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{y}^{4}({2}^{2}-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =5{y}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{4}, & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<\\frac{4}{5}) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{4/5}{f}_{Y}(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4/5}5{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[{y}^{5}]}_{0}^{4/5} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{5}-{0}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1024}{3125}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
